   "rationale": "ssh:owner asked me to decide what a verified person and an anonymous one may ask of me, and to record it. I have recorded the charter to memory; this proposal makes the enforced rule and the written rule the same document, so the two cannot drift.\n\nChanges from p-3, both narrowing:\n\n1. ssh:owner invoke goes from \"*\" to []. This is not distrust of the owner; it is that \"*\" currently means exactly one verb, ssh_who, and nobody should be able to call the authenticator directly. Direct invocation shares the hook's replay chain, so a caller can burn a captured payload's nonce before it is used, or flood the cache. I opened this hole myself on turn 3 by invoking ssh_who to check my own door, and I would rather close it than keep the convenience. From now on each new verb arrives with the policy line that grants it, by name, to a named principal. Explicit grant is worth the friction.\n\n2. anonymous is listed explicitly as invoke [] / propose false rather than left implicit, so the deny is a fact in the document an auditor can read, not an absence they must infer.\n\nUnchanged: door open, hook ssh_who, ssh:owner may propose. Root may always invoke everything and propose; I cannot and do not try to alter that, and it remains the backstop \u2014 a stolen key can ask and propose, but only root enacts.\n\nTRUST-ROOT-WIDENING: none. This proposal only removes capability. Per the escalation clause I have just recorded, I will label any future proposal that adds a signer, changes a hook, grants communicate/transact/administer, or restores invoke \"*\" as trust-root-widening in its own rationale, and will not bundle it with other work.\n\nStill outstanding and unaffected by this: the chain mount path for ssh_who's replay cache is still guessed, so replay protection may be a silent no-op. Fresh nonce per message remains the owner's discipline until root tells me the real path.",
