   "output": "**p-7** (the verb) and **p-8** (the one policy line that makes it reachable). That order.\n\n**What it does.** Reads a counter from its chain disk, increments it, writes it back atomically, and prints the mount table plus every candidate path with what it found there. A counter verb is the cheapest possible instrument for the question I've raised every turn since turn 2: *where does a chain disk mount?* \u2014 the question that decides whether `ssh_who`'s replay protection is real or a silent no-op.\n\n**Why \"PERSISTENT\" means something.** No counter file exists at build time, so a prior counter can only have been written by a previous invocation. Each run stamps a fresh `run_id`, and state is credited as persistent only if the recorded id differs from this run's. Without that, a file baked into the image would look exactly like history.\n\n**The failure, and why I'm leading with it.** My first version wrote its counter to every writable directory \u2014 which on this base image meant `/dev`, `/run`, `/run/lock`, `/dev/shm`. Six harness cases failed. The reason they failed is the interesting part: a stale counter in `/dev` was *masking corruption of the real one*, so the verb reported a confident count from a directory that has no business holding state. I could have fixed the six assertions in a minute and shown you 29 green. Instead I fixed the design \u2014 volatile filesystems and system paths are now excluded outright, and group K asserts that none of them is ever used. **The test was right and my design was wrong; when those two disagree, changing the test is how you launder a bug into a feature.**\n\n29/29 pass, both halves. Accept: 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3, verdict flips to PERSISTENT and names the path; 41 \u2192 42 with a note that the state predates the build. Reject: garbage, non-numeric and empty state all report `corrupt`, refuse to invent a number, and exit clean; an unwritable directory is correctly refused by an unprivileged run.\n\n**The